      "id": 1,
      "text": "Camp Lejeune was named in December 1942 after 13th Commandant Lt Gen John A. Lejeune.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.militarybases.us/marines/marine-corps-base-camp-lejeune/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The base began in early 1940s when a board chose the New River area as ideal for Marine training.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.militarybases.us/marines/marine-corps-base-camp-lejeune/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Camp Lejeune spans 246 square miles, making it one of the largest Marine Corps bases in the U.S.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Corps_Base_Camp_Lejeune",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Its 14 miles of beaches are used for amphibious assault training, and its location between Wilmington and Morehead City allows fast deployments.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Corps_Base_Camp_Lejeune",
